        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90005</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Capecitabine is frequently used in metastatic breast cancer, but treatment interruptions are common in older adults. We characterized the timing and reasons for interruption in routine practice. <bold>Methods: </bold>We reviewed records for patients aged 70 years or older who initiated capecitabine between 2021 and 2025. Dose interruptions, reductions, emergency visits, and treatment duration were recorded. <bold>Results: </bold>### Key findings: Among 148 patients, 62% had at least one interruption and 41% required dose reduction. Hand-foot syndrome and diarrhea were the most frequent documented causes. Most first interruptions occurred during cycles 1 or 2. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>The first two cycles represent an important period for proactive toxicity management. **Note:** This response can be adapted to the requested journal style. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
